    {
      "message": 3,
      "state": "state_0",
      "statesRecorded": 1,
      "text": "As you move down Group 2 (alkaline earth metals), some physical and chemical properties change.\n\n1. Physical properties that change\n\nProperty\n\n\t\n\nTrend down Group 2\n\n\t\n\nWhy?\n\n\n\n\nAtomic radius (size)\n\n\t\n\nIncreases ↑\n\n\t\n\nMore electron shells are added\n\n\n\n\nDensity\n\n\t\n\nGenerally increases ↑\n\n\t\n\nAtoms become heavier, although there are exceptions\n\n\n\n\nMelting point\n\n\t\n\nChanges irregularly\n\n\t\n\nDifferent strengths of metallic bonding\n\n\n\n\nHardness\n\n\t\n\nGenerally decreases ↓\n\n\t\n\nMetallic bonding tends to weaken\n\n2. Chemical properties that change\n\nProperty\n\n\t\n\nTrend down Group 2\n\n\t\n\nWhy?\n\n\n\n\nReactivity\n\n\t\n\nIncreases ↑\n\n\t\n\nOuter electrons are easier to lose\n\n\n\n\nIonization energy\n\n\t\n\nDecreases ↓\n\n\t\n\nOuter electrons are farther from the nucleus\n\n\n\n\nReaction with water\n\n\t\n\nBecomes more vigorous\n\n\t\n\nMetals lose electrons more easily\n\n3. Why do these changes happen?\n\nThe main reason is that atoms get bigger as you move down Group 2.\n\nAtomic size increases down the group\n\nAs atoms get bigger:\n\nThey have more electron shells.\n\nTheir outer electrons are farther from the nucleus and more shielded by inner electrons.\n\nThe attraction between the nucleus and outer electrons becomes weaker.\n\nThe outer electrons become easier to lose, so reactivity increases.\n\nWhat to remember for a test\n\nDown Group 2:\n\nAtomic size increases.\n\nReactivity increases.\n\nIonization energy decreases.\n\nDensity generally increases.\n\nMelting points do not follow a consistent trend.\n\nQuick question: Why is barium more reactive than magnesium?\n\nA) Barium has more valence electrons.\n\nB) Barium's outer electrons are farther from the nucleus and easier to lose.\n\nC) Barium has fewer electron shells.",
