    "text": "Alkaline Earth Metals — Chemistry Lesson\n\nAlkaline earth metals are a family of 6 elements in Group 2 of the periodic table. They're known for being reactive metals, forming positive ions, and having two electrons in their outermost shell.\n\n1. Where are they on the periodic table?\n\nSimplified periodic table — Group 2 highlighted\n\nGroup 2 is the second column from the left. The diagram is simplified, not a complete periodic table.\n\nHere are the six elements:\n\nElement\n\n\t\n\nSymbol\n\n\t\n\nAtomic number\n\n\n\n\nBeryllium\n\n\t\n\nBe\n\n\t\n\n4\n\n\n\n\nMagnesium\n\n\t\n\nMg\n\n\t\n\n12\n\n\n\n\nCalcium\n\n\t\n\nCa\n\n\t\n\n20\n\n\n\n\nStrontium\n\n\t\n\nSr\n\n\t\n\n38\n\n\n\n\nBarium\n\n\t\n\nBa\n\n\t\n\n56\n\n\n\n\nRadium\n\n\t\n\nRa\n\n\t\n\n88\n\nMemory trick: Big Monkeys Can Steal Big Rocks — Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra.\n\n2. Why are they called alkaline earth metals?\n\nThe name has three parts:\n\nAlkaline: Many of their oxides and hydroxides are basic (alkaline).\n\nEarth: Historically, chemists called certain mineral oxides \"earths.\"\n\nMetals: They have metallic properties, such as conducting electricity.\n\n3. Why do they have similar properties?\n\nAll alkaline earth metals have 2 valence electrons, meaning two electrons in their outermost energy level.\n\nExample: Magnesium (Mg)\n\nThey tend to lose both outer electrons when reacting, forming ions with a charge of +2.\n\nFor example:\n\nM\ng\n→\nM\ng\n2\n+\n+\n2\ne\n−\nMg→Mg\n2+\n+2e\n−\n\nThis is why magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and calcium forms Ca²⁺.\n\n4. Important physical and chemical properties\n\nProperty\n\n\t\n\nWhat you need to know\n\n\n\n\nAppearance\n\n\t\n\nGenerally silvery or gray metals\n\n\n\n\nConductivity\n\n\t\n\nConduct heat and electricity\n\n\n\n\nValence electrons\n\n\t\n\n2\n\n\n\n\nIon charge\n\n\t\n\nUsually +2\n\n\n\n\nReactivity\n\n\t\n\nGenerally increases down Group 2\n\n\n\n\nHardness\n\n\t\n\nGenerally harder than Group 1 metals\n\nReactivity increases down the group\n\nLess reactive\n\nMore reactive\n\nBe\n\nMg\n\nCa\n\nSr\n\nBa\n\nRa\n\nIncreasing reactivity\n\nWhy? As you move down Group 2, atoms get larger, and the outer electrons are farther from the nucleus and more shielded by inner electrons. This generally makes them easier to remove.\n\n5. How do they react?\nReaction with water\n\nSome alkaline earth metals react with water to produce a metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas.\n\nFor example:\n\nC\na\n+\n2\nH\n2\nO\n→\nC\na\n(\nO\nH\n)\n2\n+\nH\n2\nCa+2H\n2\n\t​\n\nO→Ca(OH)\n2\n\t​\n\n+H\n2\n\t​\n\n\nCalcium + water → calcium hydroxide + hydrogen.\n\nMagnesium reacts very slowly with cold water, while calcium, strontium, and barium react more readily. Beryllium does not react appreciably with water under normal conditions.\n\nReaction with oxygen\n\nThey can react with oxygen to form metal oxides.\n\n2\nM\ng\n+\nO\n2\n→\n2\nM\ng\nO\n2Mg+O\n2\n\t​\n\n→2MgO\n\nMagnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide.\n\nMagnesium burns with an intense white light, while strontium compounds can produce red flames.\n\n6. Uses in everyday life\n\nMagnesium (Mg)\n\nUsed in lightweight alloys, electronics, and vehicles. Magnesium is also essential for living organisms.\n\nCalcium (Ca)\n\nImportant for healthy bones and teeth. Calcium compounds are also used in cement and construction.\n\nStrontium (Sr)\n\nStrontium compounds are used to produce red colors in fireworks.\n\n7. Group 1 vs. Group 2\n\nThis is an important comparison for chemistry tests.\n\nGroup 1: Alkali metals\n\n\t\n\nGroup 2: Alkaline earth metals\n\n\n\n\n1 valence electron\n\n\t\n\n2 valence electrons\n\n\n\n\nForm +1 ions\n\n\t\n\nForm +2 ions\n\n\n\n\nGenerally very reactive\n\n\t\n\nGenerally less reactive\n\n\n\n\nExamples: Li, Na, K\n\n\t\n\nExamples: Be, Mg, Ca\n\n8. Test your knowledge!\n\nQuestion 2 of 4\n\n1 correct\n\nWhat charge does a calcium ion usually have?\n\n+1\n\n+2\n\n−1\n\n−2\n\nPrevious\nNext\n\nThe three most important facts to remember: Alkaline earth metals are in Group 2, have two valence electrons, and generally become more reactive as you move down the group.",
